@page
@model Parley.Web.Pages.Projects.NamingModel
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = $"Naming {Model.Project}";
}

<h1>Naming @Model.Project</h1>

<p>
    A project name is a fact about this machine and nothing else. SPEC.md §4.1 gives the protocol
    no concept of a project — membership is which endpoint an agent connected to — so renaming one
    changes what your commands call a deployment and reaches nothing on the wire. No agent
    notices, no history moves, nothing restarts.
</p>

<p>
    The console will not do it. This machine's manifest is read by two programs and written by
    exactly one, and that one is <code>parley</code>; a rename button here would make the console
    a second writer of the file whose safety comes from having a single one.
</p>

@if (Model.Named)
{
    <h2>Rename it</h2>

    <p>
        There is no single rename command yet, so it is two: forget the old name, then add the new
        one with the same paths. <code>remove</code> deletes nothing — not the store, not a
        conversation, not an agent's registration — it only forgets how this machine reaches the
        deployment.
    </p>

    <pre><code>parley project remove @Model.Project
parley project add &lt;new name&gt;</code></pre>

    <p>
        The new project gets its own folder, its own store and a port of its own — there is nothing
        to copy across. <strong>What it does not get is this project's conversations</strong>: they
        are in the old store, which <code>remove</code> leaves exactly where it is. Come back to
        <a href="/">the project list</a> and the new name is there.
    </p>
}
else
{
    <h2>Name it</h2>

    <p>
        This project is not in a manifest at all. Nothing on this machine has named a deployment,
        so the console is offering the broker your other commands already talk to, under a stand-in
        name. Give it a real one:
    </p>

    <pre><code>parley project add &lt;name&gt;</code></pre>

    <p>
        Naming it is the whole of creating it: the project gets a folder of its own, a store inside
        it, and a port claimed for it. <strong>It will not be the broker above</strong> — that one
        was started by hand or by an older entry, and it keeps its own store until it is migrated.
        Once named, the new project appears on <a href="/">the project list</a> in place of the
        stand-in.
    </p>
}
